<section class="slide" data-i="3">
      <div class="eyebrow">Slide 4 · Our method</div>
      <h2 class="section">What we changed vs plain BPE</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        Plain BPE spends <strong>all 10,000 slots</strong> building short pieces from letters.
        We only do that for about a quarter of the list. The rest is spent listing
        <strong>full words</strong> so those words cost <strong>one piece</strong> instead of three or four.
        We also decide <em>which language’s words</em> get listed first so no language stays above 1.2 pieces per word.
      </p>

      <!-- side-by-side plain comparison -->
      <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top:18px;">
        <div class="card" style="border-top: 4px solid #9e9ea0;">
          <h3>Plain BPE (what we started from)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Start with every letter as a piece.</li>
            <li>Repeatedly glue the most common neighbouring pair into a bigger piece.</li>
            <li>Keep going until the list has <strong>exactly 10,000</strong> pieces.</li>
            <li>At read time, every word is cut into those pieces — even words you see all the time on the page.</li>
            <li>English, Hindi, Telugu and Sanskrit all compete for the same merges. English shows up more often, so it wins more of the list. Smaller languages get cut into more pieces.</li>
          </ul>
          <p style="margin-top:12px;"><strong>On our pages:</strong> English X ≈ 1.22 (over 1.2). Telugu ≈ 1.64. Sanskrit ≈ 1.87. Score ≈ 1,500.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card" style="border-top: 4px solid #111;">
          <h3>Our tokenizer (what we submit)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Still use normal BPE — but only for the first <strong>~2,500</strong> slots (letters + short pieces).</li>
            <li>Use the remaining <strong>~7,500</strong> slots to store complete words that matter on the training pages.</li>
            <li>If a word is in that list, reading it costs <strong>one</strong> piece — no cutting.</li>
            <li>If a word is new or rare, fall back to the short BPE pieces — same idea as plain BPE.</li>
            <li>While building the word list: if any language still needs more than 1.2 pieces per word on average, the next free slots go to <strong>that</strong> language’s words first.</li>
          </ul>
          <p style="margin-top:12px;"><strong>On our pages:</strong> all four languages under 1.2. English X ≈ 1.16. Score ≈ 9,298.</p>
        </div>
