          <h3>Change 1 — Stop BPE early</h3>
          <p>
            Plain BPE would burn all 10,000 slots on short pieces.
            We stop the join process around 2,500 so we still have room to store complete words.
            Those short pieces remain for anything not on our word list.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Change 2 — Dictionary of full words</h3>
          <p>
            We copy whole words from the training pages into the vocabulary.
            That turns a multi-piece word into a single piece.
            Fewer pieces per word brings X down — especially for English toward the 1.2 limit.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Change 3 — Fair turn-taking</h3>
          <p>
            English has far more words, so a blind word list would mostly help English.
            If Telugu or Sanskrit (or any language) is still over 1.2 pieces per word,
            we add <em>that</em> language’s words first until it is under the bar.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout" style="margin-top:16px;">
        <strong>In one sentence:</strong>
        we keep normal letter-joining only for the hard / rare cases,
        spend most of the 10,000 list on whole words so common text is cheap,
        and always fix the language that is furthest above 1.2 pieces per word first.
      </div>
