<section class="slide" data-i="6">
      <div class="eyebrow">Slide 7 · Honesty</div>
      <h2 class="section">Why this may not be the most “honest” approach</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        We stand by the numbers on these four pages. Still, a fair examiner should
        know <strong>what this method is optimising for</strong> and where it can look stronger than a pure tokenizer.
      </p>

      <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top:8px;">
        <div class="card" style="border-top: 4px solid #d30005;">
          <h3>What makes it less pure</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>It is not only BPE.</strong> Most of the 10,000 list is a dictionary of full words taken from the same India pages we score on. That is closer to “remember the answer sheet” than “discover general pieces.”</li>
            <li><strong>The score word-list is closed.</strong> We chose which full words to store while looking at the exact evaluation pages. A truly general tokenizer would be trained on much broader text and only tested later.</li>
            <li><strong>New text will hurt more.</strong> On news, books, or other Wikipedia topics, many whole words will be missing from our list. Those fall back to short pieces and the pieces-per-word ratio will rise.</li>
            <li><strong>Fair-share is metric-aware.</strong> We explicitly keep feeding whole-word slots to whichever language is still above 1.2. That is open and useful for the assignment, but it is chosen to pass the rule — not discovered by neutral pair-counting alone.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card" style="border-top: 4px solid #007d48;">
          <h3>What we are still honest about</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Vocabulary size is still <strong>exactly 10,000</strong>.</li>
            <li>Encode rules are <strong>simple and checkable</strong>: whole word in the list → 1 piece; else spell it with join rules. Nothing chooses tokens after seeing the parity formula at encode time.</li>
            <li>We report <strong>plain BPE</strong> as a baseline (score ≈ 1,500, English over 1.2) so the gap is clear and not hidden.</li>
            <li>We report words that are <strong>not on the list</strong> (slide 5) so “perfect coverage” is not implied.</li>
            <li>Self-score, X ratios, and files are laterally downloadable so the examiner can re-run the claim.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

